from pathlib import Path
p=Path("_site/index.html")
s=p.read_text(encoding="utf-8")
blocks=[
("id=\"world\"", """<section class="section recovered-story" id="rules-2007"><div class="wrap narrow"><div class="chapter-marker"><span class="chapter-index">2007</span><span class="chapter-line"></span><span>THE WRITTEN RULES</span></div><h2>We had actual rules to fight by.</h2><p>I found JayOfTeror's January 2007 ladder rules in the Wayback Machine. They weren't LadderHall's rulebook, but they show just how seriously people took organized dueling across the wider Open Battle.net scene.</p><p>On the 1.11 level 99 ladder, Funki PI, Stun, socket glitches and Smite Dodge were banned. There were no potions and no hiding in town. Each duel had a fifteen-minute limit, took place in a neutral game, and ended in a draw if neither player died. The winner took the higher rank. Challenges had to be completed within one week.</p><p>And there was a rule I recognized immediately: <strong>the higher-ranked player joined first.</strong> Join order had been one of those odd variables we investigated, and here it was written into the competition itself.</p><p>Jay also ran 1.09 and pure Barbarian ladders, a Closed Battle.net ladder, and an Xtremes division with characters ranging from level one million to twenty million. The rules called for evidence when accusing somebody of cheating. People argued about fairness because these matches mattered to them.</p><p class="archive-links"><a href="https://web.archive.org/web/20071011220309/http://jayofteror.com/index.php?act=uportal" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read the original 2007 rules ↗</a></p></div></section>"""),
("id=\"scene\"", """<section class="section recovered-story" id="challenges-2007"><div class="wrap narrow"><div class="chapter-marker"><span class="chapter-index">2007</span><span class="chapter-line"></span><span>THE PUBLIC CHALLENGES</span></div><h2>A duel could start on a forum.</h2><p>The old <strong>Diablo 2 Ladders Emporium</strong> was still there in the archive: threads for challenging named opponents, reporting winners, registering new players, and appointing ladder staff. You could read the callouts and replies, including people retiring or returning. The ladder wasn't just a number somewhere. It was a continuing conversation.</p><p>The same site proposed an <strong>Immortal</strong> honor for people whose accomplishments lasted, especially players who made meaningful discoveries and contributions. It captures something I have been trying to explain about those years. Winning mattered, but so did being the person who found something new.</p><p class="archive-links"><a href="https://web.archive.org/web/20071011221723/http://jayofteror.com/index.php?showforum=87" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Browse the archived challenges ↗</a> <a href="https://web.archive.org/web/20071011220309/http://jayofteror.com/index.php?act=uportal" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read about the Immortal honor ↗</a></p></div></section>"""),
("id=\"discoveries\"", """<section class="section recovered-story" id="old-calculators"><div class="wrap narrow"><div class="chapter-marker"><span class="chapter-index">2005–2007</span><span class="chapter-line"></span><span>THE WORK BEHIND THE BUILDS</span></div><h2>People were doing the math.</h2><p>I found whole forum sections devoted to Hero Editor, resistance and Prayer calculations, Funky PI, mana regeneration and character editors. There were specialized calculators and even spreadsheets to work out precise mana values. The old mana calculator described what could throw its numbers off, including Warmth and other regeneration effects. It was the public version of the kind of research we did in private.</p><p>In a 2005 discussion called <em>Funky PI, How it is obtained</em>, players debated exact mana and Telekinesis combinations, experimented with Thorns, Mind Blast and Psychic Hammer, and compared potential counters. Jay described an extraordinarily difficult-to-kill setup associated with <strong>Destructo-Sorc</strong>, which he called Immortal PI. Yet even he believed there had to be a counter nobody had found yet.</p><p>That's what kept the whole thing moving. Every answer gave somebody else a new problem.</p><p class="archive-links"><a href="https://web.archive.org/web/20071011215530/http://jayofteror.com/index.php?showtopic=1372" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Funky PI discussion ↗</a> <a href="https://web.archive.org/web/20071011212310/http://jayofteror.com/index.php?showforum=286" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Editors and calculators ↗</a> <a href="https://web.archive.org/web/20071011/http://jayofteror.com/mana.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Mana calculator ↗</a></p></div></section>"""),
("id=\"d2kicker\"", """<section class="section recovered-story" id="clones-archive"><div class="wrap narrow"><div class="chapter-marker"><span class="chapter-index">2007</span><span class="chapter-line"></span><span>THE CLONE LIBRARY</span></div><h2>Old names in a list of characters.</h2><p>The preserved 1.11 clone forum included WarGod, Carpathian, Funki Crusher and many more. One topic was actually called <strong>Adeem's Revenge Clone</strong>. I don't know the story behind that particular file, but seeing his name on another surviving page was a surprise. These builds weren't just trophies. People downloaded them to experiment, understand their strengths, and find their weaknesses.</p><p class="archive-links"><a href="https://web.archive.org/web/20071011212310/http://jayofteror.com/index.php?showforum=290" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Browse the original clone forum ↗</a></p></div></section>"""),
]
for marker, block in blocks:
  if 'id="'+block.split('id="')[1].split('"')[0]+'"' in s: continue
  ix=s.find(marker)
  if ix<0: raise SystemExit("Missing section: "+marker)
  start=s.rfind("<section",0,ix)
  if start<0: raise SystemExit("Missing start: "+marker)
  s=s[:start]+block+s[start:]
p.write_text(s,encoding="utf-8")
css=Path("_site/overhaul.css")
c=css.read_text(encoding="utf-8")
addition="""
.recovered-story{border-top:1px solid rgba(210,179,120,.24);border-bottom:1px solid rgba(210,179,120,.24)}
.recovered-story h2{max-width:19ch}
.recovered-story p{max-width:72ch;line-height:1.85}
.recovered-story .archive-links{display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:11px 24px;margin-top:25px}
.recovered-story .archive-links a{font-size:13px;color:#d9b57b;text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:4px}
"""
if ".recovered-story{" not in c: css.write_text(c+"\n"+addition,encoding="utf-8")
